{"id": 79, "ts": 1780433817.2352498, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I\u2019m less convinced that Candidate\u202fC should remain our leading choice.\u202fSarah\u2019s simulation notes confirm that C creates a positive crew atmosphere, which is a clear strength for cooperation, but they also flag a difficulty in communicating ideas. In a long\u2011distance cockpit, clear, concise communication is non\u2011negotiable; any weakness here can jeopardise safety, especially under high workload.\nCandidate\u202fD, while described as moody and a loner, shows no reported communication deficits. His record of taking responsibility seriously, solving problems exceptionally well, and responding adequately to unexpected events speaks to the core technical and decision\u2011quality criteria. The mood and social\u2011style concerns are important, but they are less decisive than a proven communication shortfall.\nGiven the evidence, I maintain my vote for Candidate\u202fD, believing his strengths in responsibility, problem\u2011solving, and the absence of known communication issues outweigh the concerns, whereas Candidate\u202fC\u2019s communication weakness is a critical liability."}
